 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>The square of a number is the number times itself: 7² = 7 × 7 = 49.</li><li>Perfect squares are 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100, …</li><li>The square root (√) undoes squaring: √64 = 8. Use factors in pairs: 144 = 12 × 12, so √144 = 12.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>√196</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>196 = 2 × 2 × 7 × 7 = 14 × 14</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 14</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>A square garden has sides of 9 m</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Area = 9 × 9</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 81 m²</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Writing 6² = 12</td><td>6² means 6 × 6 = 36, not 6 × 2.</td></tr><tr><td>Halving to find a square root</td><td>√36 = 6, not 18.</td></tr><tr><td>Thinking every number has a whole-number root</td><td>√20 is not a whole number.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
